version: "1.5"
abstract: >-
  Paper, data and reproducible code testing whether a street corner of the Glasgow of 1877
  matches Don Rosa's shoeshine scene, and how often such a match would arise by chance.
  Every corner of a declared window on the 1857-58 Ordnance Survey 1:500 plan is censused.
  The drawn relations leave 8 corners with the handedness of the inked corner view (14 without);
  in the 1877-78 Post Office directory one of them, Jamaica Street x Howard Street NE, has a
  clothier fronting the side street and a spirit dealer across it. The pair occurs at 3 of the
  30 corners of the window, one of them the later Paisley's corner, which fails the drawn
  geometry; at least one survivor would carry it 62% of the time by chance, and the evidence
  raises the odds that Rosa drew a real corner of this window by a factor of 1.2-1.5.
